  body: "# Household membership for DMG living-together assessment\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nFor the captured
    living-together assessment, household membership is distinguished from sharing a dwelling and is a condition
    considered before deciding whether people are living together as a married couple.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\
    \nA shared address, bill, accommodation or caring arrangement is not by itself a couple determination. This
    is the chapter’s context-specific household concept, not an equivalence with housing, census or Universal Credit
    household definitions.\n\n## Inspected source passages\n\n- [DMG 11051, PDF page 7](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a820c1ae5274a2e8ab5738a/dmgch11.pdf#page=7)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 11: Living together as husband and wife or as civil partners .\n- [DMG 11052, PDF page 7](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a820c1ae5274a2e8ab5738a/dmgch11.pdf#page=7)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 11: Living together as husband and wife or as civil partners .\n- [DMG 11054, PDF page 7](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a820c1ae5274a2e8ab5738a/dmgch11.pdf#page=7)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 11: Living together as husband and wife or as civil partners .\n- [DMG 11056, PDF page 8](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a820c1ae5274a2e8ab5738a/dmgch11.pdf#page=8)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 11: Living together as husband and wife or as civil partners .\n\n## Evidence and review status\n\
    \nModel-assisted interpretation of the 15 September 2026 source snapshot; unreviewed and requiring specialist
    review. The source is DWP guidance, not a consolidated statement of legislation. This is an independent experimental
    publication, not an official DWP document, an entitlement decision or an executable benefits rule.\n\nPDF page
    numbers are one-based file positions. Machine extraction can alter reading order, footnotes and characters.
    Quoted relationships preserve extracted text; no visual or specialist acceptance is claimed. Source dates, legal
    effects and current applicability remain separate review tasks.\n\n- `dmg-vol3-ch11` PDF SHA-256: `1f83a691baec9f1ff9f946289647f53b1b4b3b91788c4f39f71983b136382289`;
    extracted pages SHA-256: `73fbd1921b0dcf803d66aab7915a768921372438265b337362b080284bc6e59f`.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/dmg-ltamc-assessment
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The source requires household membership but explicitly says that membership does not
      necessarily mean the people are LTAMC.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch11/0007
      quote: "11051   Two people who are neither married to each other nor a civil partner of each other\n     \
        \   must be members of the same household if they are to be treated as LTAMC and\n        thus a couple."
      locator: DMG 11051
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch11/0008
      quote: "11056   The fact that two people, who are not married to each other, are members of the\n        same
        household does not necessarily mean that they are LTAMC and so a couple.\n        A relationship may resemble
        LTAMC but consideration of its origins may show it to\n        be something quite different. Additionally,
        DMs should consider\n                                                                                  \
        \               1\n        1.    the facts and circumstances that exist while the two people are living
        together\n              and\n\n        2.    what their future plans are.\n                            \
        \                                                            1 R(G) 1/79\n\n                           \
        \                                  1\n        Such a relationship may not be classed as LTAMC , for example,
        the need for care\n        or support is the main reason that the two people are living together. It is
        not only the\n        reason that caused the parties to live together which is important, but the facts
        and\n        circumstances that apply after they have done so (see DMG 11101 – DMG 11120\n        for further
        guidance).\n                                                                                      1 R(SB)
        35/85"
      locator: DMG 11056
